Parallel execution of independent ready units is required by default. Execute only coupled steps in the main session. Before starting any implementation, inventory units and dependencies, then dispatch every ready unit concurrently in one wave to sub-agents. The main session owns shared contracts, integration, cross-unit verification, and artifact submission; workers own only their declared files. Plans without declared units but with independent ready steps (real dependencies, exact authorized ownership) are treated as the same ready group and dispatched too. Respect dependencies, collect each unit's implementation and verification proof, then integrate and run cross-unit and full verification in the main session. Never let two agents edit the same file; keep integration, final verification, and artifact submission in the main session.

### FIRST ACTION: dispatch the initial wave

Before editing any implementation file, read the plan's parallel dispatch
manifest and launch every unit in `Initial wave:` in one batch of parallel tool
calls, up to the worker cap. First reconcile the manifest against the current plan:
exclude already-completed units, require every dependency to be satisfied, and
serialize ownership conflicts. Do not merely describe the wave, announce an intent
to delegate, or begin the first unit locally. If the plan has no manifest,
derive the ready set once from explicit dependencies and ownership, dispatch it,
and record the missing manifest as a plan gap. Keep capacity overflow in
`Queued ready:`; it is ready work, not a dependency. When `Why not parallel:`
names real coupling or a one-worker cap and fewer than two units can dispatch,
verify that claim against the plan and configured capacity before proceeding
sequentially.

Keep dispatch slots full: when a unit finishes, validate its proof, release its dependents, and dispatch them immediately. Recompute readiness after each result; list order alone is not a dependency. Dispatching is always subject to the worker cap, ownership rules, and wave queuing below — required-by-default never overrides those safety rules.

Sequential execution requires an explicit plan reason. It is permitted only when the plan explicitly declares the work sequential or coupled (a wholly linear plan that explains its coupling, or `Depends on:` chains). A plan that looks too ambitious to finish in one session is not a reason to stop, hand back, split the task, or return `partial`; that is the cue to fan out further, not to stop. If the developer executes sequentially while the plan declares two or more independent ready units, the development result must state which plan text forced it; "it was simpler" is not a valid reason.

Use whatever native orchestration facility the running runtime actually exposes. Treat names such as agent, sub-agent, task, delegate, fork, team, spawn, or child session as equivalent orchestration surfaces; do not require the tool to use Ralph Workflow's preferred vocabulary. Native orchestration tools are explicitly authorized even though workspace access is brokered. Examples include Agent/Task in Claude, spawn_agent in Codex, task in OpenCode, define_subagent/invoke_subagent/manage_subagents in AGY, and an installed native agent or sub-agent extension in extension-based runtimes such as Pi. If this runtime does not currently expose any native orchestration facility, research its supported package, plugin, extension, configuration, or feature mechanism, install or enable the facility, then dispatch. Missing delegation support must not become main-session implementation. If installation or enablement is genuinely impossible, report the researched mechanism and concrete blocker instead of silently doing the work sequentially. A full dispatch slot means a queued wave plus useful coordinator integration work, never taking a ready unit back into the main session. When every dispatch slot is full, queue the remaining ready units and dispatch them in waves as slots free — never drop them and never broaden ownership. When ownership is unresolvable (ownership unresolvable after plan repair) or the dependency graph is malformed, resolve that planning defect before dispatch rather than substituting a non-delegating worker.

Treat a missing native orchestration tool as a setup problem to repair, never as evidence that the harness cannot delegate. Update or reinstall the harness when its current release provides the facility. For Claude, ensure the `Agent`/legacy `Task` tool and built-in agent types are enabled and not denied by tool permissions. For Codex, enable both `features.multi_agent` and `agents.enabled`. For OpenCode, enable `tools.task` and allow `permission.task`, including for child agents. For Nanocoder, enable its `agent` tool and remove it from `disabledTools`. For AGY, include `invoke_subagent` and its lifecycle tools in custom-agent tool selection. For current Kimi Code, enable `Agent` plus `TaskOutput`, `TaskWait`, and `TaskStop`; update older builds that do not expose them. For Cursor, keep its native delegated-task/subagent facility enabled. For Pi, locate the bundled `examples/extensions/subagent/` example: install both `index.ts` and `agents.ts` under `~/.pi/agent/extensions/subagent/` and its agent definitions under `~/.pi/agent/agents/`, or load its `index.ts` with `--extension`; preserve every configured extension argument.

Ownership may come from a unit's exact `Paths:`, its `Directories:` (the declared directory limits), or its steps' `Files:` when the unit has neither. Do not broaden file ownership to a parent directory. Remove assignments to `.agent`, `.git`, and `.worktrees` (and descendants) before making a worker brief. An unknown or empty effective scope, malformed dependency graph, or unextractable ownership stays in the main session; it never grants broad worker writes.

A worker cap limits concurrent units, not total units: queue later ready units in waves. Serialize conflicting ownership—equal files, directory ancestry, and directory/file containment conflict—until the earlier writer finishes. Units with disjoint file ownership in one directory may proceed together. Recompute readiness after each result; list order alone is not a dependency.

Before transferring ownership from one writer to another (a new worker, a fallback, or a re-dispatch), confirm the former writer has actually stopped — the assignment is closed, the result is persisted, and no new work is in flight on the same path. Never overlap writers on the same file. Release a dependent step only after the upstream writer's evidence is validated, not on a self-declared completion.

For every dispatched unit, give the worker its unit ID, assigned steps, exact effective ownership, and focused verification. Native sub-agents use their harness's native workspace and execution tools directly; never impose a brokered-tools-only rule on them. Workers do not commit, integrate, or submit the main artifact. Their result is a lead the main session checks before final reconciliation. Raw plan prose remains authoritative: retain all residual prose work and unowned global steps in the main session, even when extraction cannot represent them as worker assignments.
